=====================================================================
MASTER BUILD PROMPT — GE_Sky: From Regional Prototype to a Global,
Production-Ready, Multi-Vendor Commerce & Marketing Platform
=====================================================================

CONTEXT
You are working in an existing monorepo (Turborepo, npm workspaces):
- apps/web        → Next.js 14 App Router, TypeScript, Tailwind, Zustand, lucide-react
- apps/api        → NestJS 10 + Fastify adapter, Prisma ORM, PostgreSQL, passport-jwt, bcrypt
- packages/shared → shared TS types/utilities
- docker-compose.yml provides local Postgres, Redis, Meilisearch, Mailhog (dev only, not wired
  into the app yet)
- netlify.toml currently builds and deploys ONLY apps/web via @netlify/plugin-nextjs. apps/api
  is not deployed anywhere yet and next.config.mjs rewrites /api/v1/* to http://localhost:4000
  (a dev-only placeholder).
- The current product is Algeria-only (DZD currency, Arabic/French only, cash-on-delivery
  only, one shipping carrier), single-tenant-per-merchant, and the web app has ZERO real API
  calls — login/register/dashboard pages are disconnected mockups over local component state.

MISSION
Turn this into a real, deployable, internationally-usable multi-vendor commerce and marketing
platform — comparable in capability to Amazon (marketplace mechanics) and Ouedkniss (frictionless
listing/classifieds UX) — not a bigger prototype. Every phase below must end with something that
actually runs end-to-end (DB → API → UI), is covered by at least basic automated tests, and is
demoable. Do not skip ahead to later phases before a phase's acceptance criteria are met. Work
phase by phase, and within each phase, backend/database work before the frontend that depends on it.

GLOBAL GROUND RULES (apply to every phase)
1. Never introduce a hardcoded secret, default password, or fallback signing key. All secrets
   come from environment variables; fail fast at startup if a required one is missing.
2. Every new mutating endpoint must: validate input via a typed DTO (class-validator,
   whitelist: true, forbidNonWhitelisted: true), enforce tenant/vendor/customer ownership on
   the query itself (never trust a bare :id without a WHERE tenantId/ownerId clause), and apply
   RBAC via the existing RolesGuard + @Roles() decorator pattern in
   apps/api/src/common/guards/roles.guard.ts.
3. No client-side secret storage. Access tokens and refresh tokens live only in httpOnly,
   Secure, SameSite=Strict cookies — never localStorage/sessionStorage.
4. Every new Prisma model needs an actual migration (prisma migrate dev / deploy), not just
   `db push`, so history is reproducible in CI/CD.
5. Every phase must update or add automated tests (unit for services, e2e for controllers using
   NestJS's Test module + Supertest, component/integration tests for web using
   Vitest/Playwright) — do not mark a phase done without them.
6. Keep apps/web and apps/api independently deployable. apps/web must talk to apps/api only via
   a configurable NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL / server-side env var — never a hardcoded localhost URL.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 0 — Security & Correctness Baseline (prerequisite for everything else)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Fix these known defects in the existing apps/api code before building on top of it:
- apps/api/src/main.ts: replace `enableCors({ origin: true, credentials: true })` with an
  explicit allowlist driven by an ALLOWED_ORIGINS env var.
- apps/api/src/modules/auth/jwt.strategy.ts and auth.module.ts: remove the hardcoded fallback
  JWT secret string entirely; throw on boot if JWT_ACCESS_SECRET (and a separate
  JWT_REFRESH_SECRET) is not set.
- apps/api/prisma/seed.ts: gate seeding behind NODE_ENV !== 'production', and never seed a
  known password into anything that could become a real environment.
- apps/api/src/modules/integrations/integrations.service.ts: generate API keys with
  crypto.randomBytes(32), store only a bcrypt/argon2 hash (or HMAC) of the secret, and return
  the raw secret to the caller exactly once at creation time.
- apps/api/src/modules/tenants/tenants.service.ts updateTenant(): replace the raw `data: any`
  passthrough with an explicit UpdateTenantDto that whitelists only branding/business fields
  (name, logo, address, contact info) — plan/status must never be settable from this endpoint.
- Apply RolesGuard + @Roles('OWNER','ADMIN') to: tenant settings update, team management,
  billing, integrations/API key management.
- Fix the ownership-check gap in integrations.service.ts toggleConnection(), automation.service.ts
  toggleWorkflowStatus(), and agents.service.ts updateTaskStatus() — scope every update's WHERE
  clause by tenantId (follow the pattern already used correctly in products.service.ts findOne()
  and notifications.service.ts updateMany()).
- Add @nestjs/throttler on /auth/* routes.
- Add DTOs (class-validator) for register/login/all create-and-update endpoints currently typed
  as `@Body() body: any`.
Acceptance: `npm audit`-relevant classes of bugs above are gone from a code review; e2e tests
prove cross-tenant update attempts return 403/404; login/register reject malformed input.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 1 — Real Authentication & Session Lifecycle (login/logout, done properly)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Backend (apps/api):
- Split access tokens (short-lived, ~15 min) from refresh tokens (7-30 days), each with a
  `type` claim ('access' | 'refresh') that JwtStrategy explicitly checks — a refresh token must
  never validate against a protected resource endpoint.
- Implement refresh token ROTATION: store refresh tokens hashed in a new RefreshToken Prisma
  model (id, userId, tokenHash, familyId, revokedAt, expiresAt, createdAt). POST /auth/refresh
  validates the incoming token, revokes it, issues a new pair in the same family, and on reuse
  of an already-revoked token, revokes the entire family and forces re-login on all devices.
- POST /auth/logout revokes the current refresh token (and optionally the whole family for
  "log out everywhere").
- Add POST /auth/register email verification (signed token emailed via a mail provider —
  wire Mailhog locally, a real provider like Resend/Postmark/SES in production).
- Add POST /auth/forgot-password and /auth/reset-password with short-lived signed reset tokens.
- Add optional TOTP-based 2FA using the existing (unused) `twoFactorSecret` field on User —
  otplib or speakeasy for enrollment/verification.
- Add OAuth social login (Google at minimum, Apple/Facebook as stretch) via
  @nestjs/passport strategies, linked to the same User/Tenant model.
- Set access + refresh tokens as httpOnly/Secure/SameSite cookies in the response, not JSON body.
Frontend (apps/web):
- Replace the fake setTimeout logic in apps/web/src/app/login/page.tsx and register/page.tsx
  with real calls to the API (server actions or a typed API client in packages/shared).
- Add a proper auth guard for everything under apps/web/src/app/app/** (redirect to /login if
  no valid session) using Next.js middleware that checks the httpOnly cookie server-side.
- Add a real logout action that calls /auth/logout and clears cookies/state.
- Add email verification, forgot/reset password, and 2FA enrollment UI flows.
Acceptance: a user can register, verify email, log in, be redirected on an expired/invalid
session, refresh silently, enable 2FA, and log out — with refresh-token reuse triggering a
full session revocation, verified by an e2e test.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 2 — Internationalization & Multi-Region Foundations
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
- Add a Region/Locale concept: supported locales beyond ar/fr (start with en, then expand by
  target market), driven by Next.js i18n routing (/[locale]/...) with the existing
  apps/web/src/lib/translations.ts restructured into per-locale message catalogs (or migrate
  to next-intl for pluralization/formatting correctness).
- Add a Currency model + exchange-rate sync job (e.g. via a scheduled task hitting a rates API,
  cached in Redis) so prices can be authored in a merchant's base currency and displayed
  converted (with clean, locally-appropriate rounding) in the buyer's currency.
- Add per-tenant/per-market settings for date/number formatting, timezone, and default currency
  instead of the current hardcoded 'DZD' defaults scattered across
  apps/api/src/modules/*/*.service.ts.
- Ensure all customer-facing content (product descriptions, notifications, emails) is
  locale-aware in the schema (translatable fields), not just UI chrome.
Acceptance: switching locale changes currency formatting, date formatting, and translated
content consistently across a full page (catalog, cart, checkout), not just labels.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 3 — Global Payments & Checkout
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
- Introduce a PaymentProvider abstraction in apps/api (interface + adapters) so COD, Stripe,
  and PayPal can coexist per market/tenant configuration.
- Model Order payment state machine explicitly (pending → authorized → captured → refunded/
  failed) with webhook handlers that verify provider signatures (Stripe webhook signing
  secret, PayPal webhook verification) — do not trust unauthenticated webhook payloads.
- Add tax/VAT/duty calculation per shipping destination (a pluggable TaxCalculator interface;
  start with a simple rate table per country, allow swapping in a service like TaxJar/Avalara
  later).
- Frontend: real checkout flow with saved payment methods, multi-currency price display, and
  clear breakdown of taxes/duties before payment.
Acceptance: a test purchase completes through at least one real payment provider in test mode,
with a verified webhook updating order status, and a full refund flow working end-to-end.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 4 — Multi-Vendor Marketplace Data Model
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
- Extend the Prisma schema: introduce a Vendor entity distinct from Tenant (a Tenant can host
  many Vendors in marketplace mode, or remain single-vendor for direct merchants) with its own
  catalog scoping, commission rate, and payout ledger.
- Add vendor onboarding flow (application → verification → approval) and a vendor-facing
  dashboard (reuse/extend apps/web/src/app/app/** patterns under a new vendor portal route).
- Support order splitting: a single customer checkout can produce multiple sub-orders (one per
  vendor) with independent fulfillment/status tracking, rolled up into one customer-facing order.
- Add a Payout model + scheduled payout job (per vendor, per currency, minus commission).
- Add product reviews/ratings tied to verified purchases, and basic seller ratings.
Acceptance: two distinct vendor accounts under one marketplace tenant can each list products,
receive orders from a shared cart/checkout, and see independent payout ledgers.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 5 — Global Shipping & Fulfillment
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
- Replace the hardcoded 'Yalidine Express' default in apps/api/src/modules/orders/orders.service.ts
  with a ShippingProvider abstraction (interface: rate quote, label creation, tracking) with
  adapters for the existing local carrier plus at least one international carrier
  (DHL/FedEx/Aramex API sandbox).
- Add rate shopping (query multiple providers, pick/display best rate) and address validation
  per destination country.
- Add shipment tracking webhook/polling that updates Order status and notifies the customer.
Acceptance: an order can be fulfilled through either the local or an international carrier
adapter without changing order/checkout code, selected dynamically by destination.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 6 — Search, Discovery & Recommendations
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
- Actually wire up Meilisearch (already provisioned in docker-compose.yml but unused): index
  products/vendors on create/update via an event listener (NestJS EventEmitterModule is already
  installed — use it), expose a /search endpoint with full-text + facet filters (category,
  price range, vendor, rating, region availability).
- Add a basic recommendation service (start with co-purchase/"customers also bought" from order
  history; leave room to swap in a real ML service later) surfaced on product and cart pages.
- Frontend: real search bar with instant results, filters, and pagination replacing any static
  mock listings.
Acceptance: searching returns real indexed data with sub-second response and correct facet
counts; a product page shows real related-product recommendations.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 7 — Frontend Application Architecture & Navigation
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
- Replace all mock/local-only state in apps/web/src/app/app/** with real data fetching (React
  Query or Next.js Server Components/Server Actions hitting the now-real API from Phase 1-6).
- Rebuild navigation: consistent breadcrumbs, deep-linkable filters/search state in the URL,
  a global command palette (apps/web/src/components/shared/CommandPalette.tsx already exists —
  wire it to real navigable entities), and consistent loading/error/empty states everywhere.
- Add SEO fundamentals: per-page metadata, dynamic sitemap.xml, JSON-LD structured data for
  products/organization, canonical URLs per locale.
- Add responsive/mobile-first pass and basic accessibility audit (keyboard navigation, ARIA
  labels, color contrast) across all app pages.
Acceptance: every page under apps/web/src/app renders real backend data, is reachable via
consistent navigation with no dead links, and passes a Lighthouse SEO/accessibility check
above an agreed threshold.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 8 — Performance, Caching & Background Processing
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
- Wire Redis (already in docker-compose.yml, unused) as: (a) a cache layer for read-heavy
  endpoints (catalog listings, analytics dashboards), (b) the refresh-token/session blacklist
  from Phase 1, (c) the backing store for a job queue.
- Add BullMQ (Redis-backed) for background jobs: transactional emails, AI agent task execution
  (apps/api/src/modules/agents/agents.service.ts currently runs synchronously in-request),
  webhook delivery retries, payout batch jobs, search re-indexing.
- Add image optimization/CDN delivery for product images (Netlify Image CDN for the frontend,
  or a dedicated asset pipeline if vendor-uploaded images need transformation at upload time).
Acceptance: catalog/dashboard endpoints show a measurable latency drop with caching enabled;
long-running agent tasks return immediately with a job id and complete asynchronously.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 9 — Observability, Testing & CI/CD
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
- Add structured logging (pino, since Fastify is already in use) with request correlation IDs.
- Add error monitoring (Sentry or equivalent) for both apps/api and apps/web.
- Add health check endpoints (/health, /health/db, /health/redis) for the API.
- Build out a real test suite: unit tests per service, e2e tests per controller (auth flows,
  cross-tenant isolation, payment webhook signature rejection), and a handful of Playwright
  end-to-end flows for the web app (register → verify → login → browse → checkout → logout).
- Add a CI pipeline (GitHub Actions or equivalent) running lint, typecheck, tests, and
  `prisma migrate deploy --dry-run` on every PR, blocking merge on failure.
Acceptance: CI is green on a clean clone with only env vars set; a deliberately broken auth
change fails CI before merge.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 10 — Trust, Compliance & Support
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
- Add real legal pages (Terms of Service, Privacy Policy, Vendor Agreement) with per-locale
  versions, and a cookie-consent flow suitable for GDPR-covered visitors.
- Add basic fraud/abuse heuristics on checkout (velocity limits, mismatched
  billing/shipping-country risk flags) — start simple, avoid over-engineering.
- Add a customer support channel (at minimum a ticketing model tied to Customer/Order; a live
  chat widget or helpdesk integration as a stretch goal).
- Add content moderation hooks for vendor-submitted listings (basic profanity/prohibited-item
  filtering before a listing goes live in marketplace mode).
Acceptance: legal pages are locale-aware and linked from checkout/footer; a flagged checkout
attempt is visible to an admin before funds are captured.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 11 — Deployment Topology & Go-Live
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
- Decide and implement a real hosting target for apps/api (it cannot run as-is on Netlify —
  it's a long-lived Fastify server with background workers, not a set of stateless functions).
  Reasonable options: Render, Fly.io, Railway, or a container on AWS/GCP. Document the choice
  and the deployment steps.
- Point apps/web's API base URL at the real deployed API origin via environment variables (no
  more localhost rewrite in next.config.mjs for any non-local environment).
- Add security headers via netlify.toml [[headers]] (CSP scoped to actual asset/script origins,
  X-Frame-Options, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy, HSTS) once real third-party origins are
  finalized.
- Set up separate dev/staging/production environments with isolated databases and secrets, and
  a documented promotion process between them.
- Gate or remove public access to Swagger docs (apps/api/src/main.ts SwaggerModule.setup) in
  production.
Acceptance: a fresh visitor on the real production domain can complete the full register →
browse → checkout → order-tracking journey against the real deployed API, over HTTPS, with
security headers present and no console/network errors.

